1
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:15,000
Trapped outside the boundaries of time and space... 102
aircraft... 6,000 men... all missing.

2
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:24,000
The Final Countdown is a war, action, science
fiction film directed by Don Taylor.

3
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:33,000
During routine manoeuvres near Hawaii in 1980, the aircraft-carrier USS Nimitz is caught in a strange vortex-like
storm, throwing the ship back in time to 1941—mere hours before the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbor.

4
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:42,000
Capt. Matthew Yelland, played by Kirk Douglas.

5
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:51,000
Warren Lasky, played by Martin Sheen.

6
00:30:54,000 --> 00:31:00,000
Laurel Scott, played by Katharine Ross.

7
00:36:03,000 --> 00:36:09,000
Wing Commander Richard T. Owens, played by James Farentino.

8
00:41:12,000 --> 00:41:18,000
Commander Dan Thurman, played by Ron O'Neal.

9
00:46:21,000 --> 00:46:27,000
Senator Samuel Chapman, played by Charles Durning.

10
00:51:30,000 --> 00:51:36,000
The Final Countdown is a 1980 American science fiction war film about a modern, nuclear-powered aircraft
carrier that travels through time to the day before the December 7, 1941, attack on Pearl Harbor.

11
00:56:39,000 --> 00:56:45,000
It is directed by Don Taylor, his final theatrical film as director, and stars Kirk Douglas,
Martin Sheen, James Farentino, Katharine Ross, Ron O'Neal, and Charles Durning.

12
01:01:48,000 --> 01:01:54,000
The film was produced with the cooperation of the United States Navy's naval
aviation branch and the United States Department of Defense.

13
01:06:57,000 --> 01:07:03,000
It was set and filmed on board USS Nimitz (CVN-68), filming operations of the
modern, nuclear warship, which had been launched in the early 1970s.

14
01:12:06,000 --> 01:12:12,000
Troma Entertainment founder Lloyd Kaufman was an associate
producer of the film, and also has a minor acting role.

15
01:17:15,000 --> 01:17:21,000
The Final Countdown was released by United Artists on August 1, 1980.

16
01:22:24,000 --> 01:22:30,000
It received mixed reviews from critics, but was a
moderate success at the box office.

17
01:27:33,000 --> 01:27:39,000
It received Saturn Award nominations for Best Science
Fiction Film and Best Actor (for Kirk Douglas).

18
01:32:42,000 --> 01:32:48,000
The film explores themes of airplane, world war ii,
time travel, pearl harbor, pacific war.

19
01:37:51,000 --> 01:37:57,000
The tone is thought_provoking.
